1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,495
Athens. The year three hundred ninety-nine B.C.

2
00:00:03,515 --> 00:00:05,959
An old man is about to drink poison.

3
00:00:06,328 --> 00:00:08,144
JAILER: Socrates. It's time.

4
00:00:08,464 --> 00:00:10,536
SOCRATES: Then bring the cup, my friend.

5
00:00:10,556 --> 00:00:12,070
And don't look so sad.

6
00:00:12,441 --> 00:00:14,031
YOU: Teacher, please! There's still time.

7
00:00:14,051 --> 00:00:15,240
We can get you out tonight!

8
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:18,429
That's you. And to understand why he won't run,

9
00:00:18,449 --> 00:00:20,387
we need to go back to the day you

10
00:00:20,407 --> 00:00:20,981
were born.

11
00:00:23,201 --> 00:00:26,988
Four hundred thirty-five B.C. You're born in the potters'

12
00:00:27,008 --> 00:00:30,060
quarter of Athens, the richest city in Greece.

13
00:00:30,431 --> 00:00:31,775
FATHER: A boy! Ten days from now,

14
00:00:31,795 --> 00:00:34,013
we name him in front of the whole family.

15
00:00:34,334 --> 00:00:36,473
MOTHER: And one day, he'll vote in the Assembly.

16
00:00:36,493 --> 00:00:37,121
Not like me.

17
00:00:37,442 --> 00:00:40,222
She's right. In this famous democracy,

18
00:00:40,242 --> 00:00:42,727
only adult male citizens can vote.

19
00:00:42,747 --> 00:00:45,306
Women, slaves and foreigners can't.

20
00:00:45,627 --> 00:00:47,186
MOTHER: At seven, he starts school.

21
00:00:47,206 --> 00:00:49,116
Letters, the lyre, and wrestling.

22
00:00:49,436 --> 00:00:51,336
FATHER: A slave walks him there and back.

23
00:00:51,356 --> 00:00:52,324
Every single day.

24
00:00:52,645 --> 00:00:54,422
YOU: And my sister? Does she go too?

25
00:00:54,692 --> 00:00:56,230
MOTHER: She learns at home. Weaving,

26
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:58,177
and running a house. That's Athens.

27
00:00:59,097 --> 00:01:01,065
FATHER: Then he'd better learn to speak well.

28
00:01:01,085 --> 00:01:02,623
Words win votes in this city.

29
00:01:04,843 --> 00:01:08,070
When you're five, a plague hits the crowded city.

30
00:01:08,090 --> 00:01:10,322
Up to a quarter of the people die,

31
00:01:10,342 --> 00:01:12,906
even Pericles, Athens' greatest leader.

32
00:01:13,275 --> 00:01:15,156
MOTHER: Stay inside. Don't touch anyone.

33
00:01:15,176 --> 00:01:16,285
The gods are angry.

34
00:01:16,704 --> 00:01:18,704
You survive. And at seventeen,

35
00:01:18,724 --> 00:01:20,925
you're running pots to the Agora,

36
00:01:20,945 --> 00:01:22,810
the beating heart of Athens.

37
00:01:23,129 --> 00:01:24,820
FATHER: Two jars for the olive seller.

38
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:27,214
And stay away from the barefoot man by the

39
00:01:27,234 --> 00:01:28,183
shoemaker's shop.

40
00:01:28,504 --> 00:01:30,022
YOU: Why? Is he dangerous?

41
00:01:30,292 --> 00:01:32,015
FATHER: Worse. He asks questions.

42
00:01:32,935 --> 00:01:35,228
YOU: Then why does everyone crowd around him?

43
00:01:35,498 --> 00:01:36,898
FATHER: Because he's fun to watch.

44
00:01:36,918 --> 00:01:38,862
Until he turns the questions on you.

45
00:01:41,082 --> 00:01:42,820
SOCRATES: Young man. You carry pots.

46
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:45,322
Tell me, what makes a pot a good pot?

47
00:01:45,641 --> 00:01:47,114
YOU: Easy. It doesn't leak.

48
00:01:47,384 --> 00:01:50,012
SOCRATES: Good! And what makes a man a good man?

49
00:01:50,331 --> 00:01:52,585
YOU: Um. He's rich? Strong? He wins arguments?

50
00:01:52,855 --> 00:01:54,510
SOCRATES: Then a rich man who lies,

51
00:01:54,530 --> 00:01:56,319
is good? Hmm. I'm not sure.

52
00:01:56,338 --> 00:01:56,855
Are you?

53
00:01:57,174 --> 00:01:58,828
YOU: I thought I knew. Now I'm not sure of

54
00:01:58,848 --> 00:01:59,234
anything.

55
00:01:59,555 --> 00:02:02,252
SOCRATES: Wonderful! That's where wisdom begins.

56
00:02:02,272 --> 00:02:03,968
My mother was a midwife.

57
00:02:03,988 --> 00:02:06,400
I help people give birth to ideas.

58
00:02:06,819 --> 00:02:10,574
He's short, snub-nosed, wears the same cloak all year,

59
00:02:10,594 --> 00:02:12,741
and never writes anything down.

60
00:02:12,761 --> 00:02:14,977
Comedy writers love mocking him.

61
00:02:15,897 --> 00:02:17,600
YOU: I saw a play about you when I was

62
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:19,739
twelve. The Clouds! You hung in a basket,

63
00:02:19,759 --> 00:02:20,730
staring at the sky!

64
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:23,066
SOCRATES: Ha! I stood up in the theater,

65
00:02:23,086 --> 00:02:26,188
so people could compare the mask with the real

66
00:02:26,208 --> 00:02:26,528
face.

67
00:02:26,848 --> 00:02:28,941
At least, that's how the legend goes.

68
00:02:31,162 --> 00:02:33,460
Soon, you follow him everywhere.

69
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:35,995
Today, he corners a famous general,

70
00:02:36,015 --> 00:02:36,502
Laches.

71
00:02:36,823 --> 00:02:39,119
SOCRATES: Laches, you've fought in battles.

72
00:02:39,139 --> 00:02:40,242
What is courage?

73
00:02:40,562 --> 00:02:43,374
LACHES: Simple. A brave man stands his ground and never

74
00:02:43,394 --> 00:02:43,675
runs.

75
00:02:43,996 --> 00:02:46,570
SOCRATES: And soldiers who pull back on purpose,

76
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:48,140
to win the fight later?

77
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:49,300
Are they cowards?

78
00:02:49,621 --> 00:02:51,034
LACHES: Well, no. Then courage is,

79
00:02:51,054 --> 00:02:52,302
uh, being tough inside?

80
00:02:52,573 --> 00:02:56,319
SOCRATES: Even tough and foolish? Is foolish stubbornness courage?

81
00:02:56,639 --> 00:02:59,144
LACHES: By Zeus, Socrates! I know what courage is.

82
00:02:59,164 --> 00:03:00,346
I just can't say it!

83
00:03:00,765 --> 00:03:03,456
That's the Socratic method. No lectures.

84
00:03:03,476 --> 00:03:07,318
Just question after question, until the weak answers fall

85
00:03:07,338 --> 00:03:07,724
apart.

86
00:03:08,044 --> 00:03:10,180
PLATO: Teacher, I came here to wrestle.

87
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:12,200
Can I stay and listen instead?

88
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:14,267
That young wrestler is Plato.

89
00:03:14,287 --> 00:03:16,521
He'll write all of this down one day.

90
00:03:16,541 --> 00:03:18,165
It's how we know any of it.

91
00:03:19,086 --> 00:03:21,503
YOU: Father, are men good because the gods love them?

92
00:03:21,523 --> 00:03:23,838
Or do the gods love them because they're good?

93
00:03:24,158 --> 00:03:25,294
FATHER: Go to your room.

94
00:03:25,715 --> 00:03:29,156
Questioning everything is not always popular at home.

95
00:03:31,377 --> 00:03:35,013
Four sixteen B.C. You're invited to a symposium.

96
00:03:35,033 --> 00:03:37,374
A drinking party, with couches,

97
00:03:37,394 --> 00:03:39,811
music and wine mixed with water.

98
00:03:40,181 --> 00:03:42,021
YOU: Why do we mix the wine with water?

99
00:03:42,292 --> 00:03:44,739
SOCRATES: Only barbarians drink it straight.

100
00:03:44,759 --> 00:03:47,786
And this way, the talking lasts till dawn.

101
00:03:48,106 --> 00:03:49,741
ALCIBIADES: Make room! Where's Socrates?

102
00:03:50,062 --> 00:03:51,887
YOU: Is that Alcibiades? The war hero?

103
00:03:51,907 --> 00:03:52,781
He's very drunk.

104
00:03:53,102 --> 00:03:55,601
ALCIBIADES: Friends, this man is like those little statues in

105
00:03:55,621 --> 00:03:57,194
the shops. Ugly on the outside.

106
00:03:57,214 --> 00:03:59,200
Open him up, and there are gods inside.

107
00:03:59,569 --> 00:04:00,926
ALCIBIADES: At Potidaea, I was wounded.

108
00:04:00,946 --> 00:04:02,709
He stood over me and saved my life.

109
00:04:02,729 --> 00:04:04,238
Then he let me take the medal!

110
00:04:04,609 --> 00:04:06,965
Everyone falls asleep before dawn.

111
00:04:06,985 --> 00:04:09,901
Socrates simply gets up, and goes to wash,

112
00:04:09,921 --> 00:04:12,417
and starts another day of questions.

113
00:04:14,638 --> 00:04:16,618
CHAEREPHON: Want to hear why he does all this?

114
00:04:16,638 --> 00:04:18,441
I went to the Oracle at Delphi,

115
00:04:18,461 --> 00:04:20,854
and asked: is anyone wiser than Socrates?

116
00:04:21,173 --> 00:04:22,575
CHAEREPHON: And the priestess said: no one!

117
00:04:22,894 --> 00:04:25,389
SOCRATES: I was puzzled. I know that I know very

118
00:04:25,409 --> 00:04:28,300
little. So I went looking for someone wiser.

119
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:33,050
SOCRATES: Politicians, poets, craftsmen. They all thought they knew everything.

120
00:04:33,070 --> 00:04:33,825
They didn't.

121
00:04:34,144 --> 00:04:36,660
SOCRATES: So perhaps I'm wiser in one small way.

122
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:39,263
I don't think I know what I don't know.

123
00:04:39,682 --> 00:04:42,978
There's one problem. Proving powerful people wrong,

124
00:04:42,997 --> 00:04:45,448
in public, makes you a lot of enemies.

125
00:04:46,367 --> 00:04:48,909
XANTHIPPE: Socrates! You talk all day and earn nothing.

126
00:04:48,929 --> 00:04:50,480
We have three sons to feed!

127
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:53,772
SOCRATES: She's right, of course. I never charge for a

128
00:04:53,792 --> 00:04:54,656
conversation.

129
00:04:54,975 --> 00:04:57,335
Unlike the paid teachers of his day,

130
00:04:57,355 --> 00:04:59,648
the sophists, he never takes money.

131
00:04:59,668 --> 00:05:01,367
His family pays the price.

132
00:05:03,586 --> 00:05:07,698
Four hundred four B.C. After twenty-seven years of war,

133
00:05:07,718 --> 00:05:11,228
Sparta beats Athens. Thirty tyrants grab power,

134
00:05:11,248 --> 00:05:12,581
and kill hundreds.

135
00:05:12,951 --> 00:05:16,028
HERALD: Socrates! Go to Salamis, arrest a man named Leon,

136
00:05:16,048 --> 00:05:17,672
and bring him here to die.

137
00:05:17,992 --> 00:05:20,381
SOCRATES: No. I won't do something unjust.

138
00:05:20,701 --> 00:05:22,682
YOU: Teacher, weren't you afraid they'd kill you?

139
00:05:22,952 --> 00:05:25,178
SOCRATES: No one knows if death is bad.

140
00:05:25,198 --> 00:05:27,501
But doing wrong? That, I know,

141
00:05:27,521 --> 00:05:28,043
is bad.

142
00:05:28,413 --> 00:05:31,580
He just walks home. The tyrants fall before they

143
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:34,302
can punish him. But some of them had been

144
00:05:34,322 --> 00:05:36,627
his students. And Athens remembers.

145
00:05:38,846 --> 00:05:41,854
Three ninety-nine B.C. Democracy is back.

146
00:05:41,874 --> 00:05:43,552
And so are the enemies.

147
00:05:43,572 --> 00:05:45,619
Five hundred and one jurors.

148
00:05:45,639 --> 00:05:47,170
One day. One verdict.

149
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:50,633
Jurors are picked at random by a stone machine

150
00:05:50,653 --> 00:05:53,746
called a kleroterion, so no one can bribe them

151
00:05:53,766 --> 00:05:54,491
in advance.

152
00:05:54,860 --> 00:05:57,139
MELETUS: Socrates does not honor the city's gods.

153
00:05:57,159 --> 00:05:58,634
And he corrupts the young!

154
00:05:58,954 --> 00:06:00,549
YOU: He means us. His students.

155
00:06:00,869 --> 00:06:03,942
SOCRATES: Meletus, tell us. Who makes the young better?

156
00:06:04,211 --> 00:06:05,151
MELETUS: The laws!

157
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:07,253
SOCRATES: And who else? The jurors?

158
00:06:07,273 --> 00:06:09,699
The Assembly? Everyone in Athens,

159
00:06:09,719 --> 00:06:10,441
except me?

160
00:06:10,711 --> 00:06:11,670
MELETUS: Exactly!

161
00:06:11,939 --> 00:06:14,002
SOCRATES: Then the young are very lucky.

162
00:06:14,022 --> 00:06:17,127
Only one corrupter, and thousands of helpers.

163
00:06:17,497 --> 00:06:21,221
SOCRATES: Men of Athens, I will never stop asking questions.

164
00:06:21,241 --> 00:06:24,217
The unexamined life is not worth living.

165
00:06:24,586 --> 00:06:27,486
HERALD: The jury has voted. Two hundred and eighty,

166
00:06:27,506 --> 00:06:29,592
guilty. Two hundred and twenty,

167
00:06:29,612 --> 00:06:30,338
not guilty.

168
00:06:30,709 --> 00:06:33,264
SOCRATES: Thirty more votes, and I'd be free.

169
00:06:33,284 --> 00:06:36,724
As punishment, I propose free dinners for life,

170
00:06:36,744 --> 00:06:38,564
like an Olympic champion.

171
00:06:38,934 --> 00:06:41,816
The jury doesn't laugh. Even more of them vote

172
00:06:41,836 --> 00:06:42,448
for death.

173
00:06:44,668 --> 00:06:46,876
CRITO: Socrates, listen. The guards are paid.

174
00:06:46,896 --> 00:06:49,340
A boat is ready. You can live in Thessaly.

175
00:06:49,660 --> 00:06:53,108
SOCRATES: Crito, I've lived seventy years under the laws of

176
00:06:53,128 --> 00:06:55,303
Athens. Shall I break them now,

177
00:06:55,323 --> 00:06:56,365
to save myself?

178
00:06:56,735 --> 00:06:59,313
XANTHIPPE: Oh Socrates! This is the last time you'll talk

179
00:06:59,333 --> 00:07:00,330
with your friends!

180
00:07:00,649 --> 00:07:03,215
SOCRATES: Crito, have someone take her home.

181
00:07:03,235 --> 00:07:03,747
Gently.

182
00:07:04,168 --> 00:07:05,785
YOU: How can you be so calm?

183
00:07:06,055 --> 00:07:08,365
SOCRATES: Death is either a dreamless sleep,

184
00:07:08,385 --> 00:07:11,312
or a journey to meet the great souls before

185
00:07:11,332 --> 00:07:13,232
us. Either way, why fear it?

186
00:07:13,601 --> 00:07:15,544
He drinks the hemlock calmly.

187
00:07:15,564 --> 00:07:18,117
His legs grow heavy, and he lies down.

188
00:07:18,487 --> 00:07:21,208
SOCRATES: Crito, we owe a rooster to Asclepius.

189
00:07:21,228 --> 00:07:22,765
Pay it. Don't forget.

190
00:07:23,185 --> 00:07:24,823
Those are his last words.

191
00:07:27,142 --> 00:07:29,139
But the questions don't die.

192
00:07:29,159 --> 00:07:31,588
Plato opens a school, the Academy.

193
00:07:31,608 --> 00:07:35,189
His student Aristotle teaches Alexander the Great.

194
00:07:35,559 --> 00:07:39,020
Today, law schools and classrooms still use the Socratic

195
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:42,314
method, nearly two thousand four hundred years later.

196
00:07:42,685 --> 00:07:44,209
YOU: He never wrote a single word.

197
00:07:44,229 --> 00:07:45,381
We wrote them for him.

198
00:07:45,750 --> 00:07:48,305
PLATO: Everything I wrote, I put in his mouth.

199
00:07:48,325 --> 00:07:51,013
He's the hero of almost all my dialogues.

200
00:07:51,382 --> 00:07:52,737
So, if you'd been there,

201
00:07:52,757 --> 00:07:54,341
would you have followed him?

202
00:07:54,361 --> 00:07:56,976
Or voted against him? Tell me in the comments.
